<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Merged into the generated Info.plist by tauri-bundler (a plist sitting next
    to tauri.conf.json is merged over the generated one).

    WHY: the first thing a user does after onboarding is pick a video, and that
    video is overwhelmingly in ~/Downloads or on the Desktop — both TCC-protected
    on macOS. The read does not even happen in this process: the shell shows the
    picker, then the orchestrator sidecar and the bundled ffmpeg read the ORIGINAL
    path (runner.ts probe/extractAudio). macOS shows a consent prompt for these
    categories with or without a usage string, but without one the prompt is a
    bare "VideoDubber would like to access files in your Downloads folder" at the
    exact moment the user expected work to start — and a user who clicks
    "Don't Allow" gets a pipeline that fails at the probe step with an ffmpeg
    error and no way back short of System Settings.

    These strings do not prevent the prompt; they explain it.
  -->
  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>VideoDubber needs access to read the video you chose so it can transcribe, translate and re-voice it on this Mac.</string>

  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>VideoDubber needs access to read the video you chose so it can transcribe, translate and re-voice it on this Mac.</string>

  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>VideoDubber needs access to read the video you chose so it can transcribe, translate and re-voice it on this Mac.</string>

  <!--
    VideoDubber is local/offline-first: everything runs on the user's machine.
    It never sends a video, a transcript or a model anywhere, so declare that
    rather than letting the default (which permits arbitrary outbound HTTP)
    stand. Downloads of models and engine packs go to HTTPS endpoints, which
    needs no exception.
  -->
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
  <dict>
    <!-- The backend is four loopback HTTP services (127.0.0.1:5100-5103); ATS
         does not apply to loopback, but be explicit about the intent. -->
    <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
